This line in THDTensor is problematic for some gcc versions. The inttypes.h include isn't enough for older compilers. It looks like you also need to #define __STDC_FORMAT_MACROS to build properly.
Here's a minimal example:
#define __STDC_FORMAT_MACROS

#include <inttypes.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("%" PRId64 "\n", 10);
    return 0;
}
commenting out the #define causes the following error with gcc 4.8.5:
test.cpp: In function ‘int main()’:
test.cpp:7:16: error: expected ‘)’ before ‘PRId64’
     printf("%" PRId64 "\n", 10);

but does work with gcc 5.4.0. I haven't tested with any other compilers.

Here is what I plan to augment the generated code with:
Tensor VariableType::transpose(const Tensor & self, int64_t dim0, int64_t dim1) const {
    auto& self_ = unpack(self, "self", 0);
    auto flags = _flags({ self });
    auto grad_fn = std::make_shared<TransposeBackward>();
    if (flags.is_executable) {
      grad_fn->dim0 = dim0;
      grad_fn->dim1 = dim1;
    }
    auto ret = as_variable(baseType->transpose(self_, dim0, dim1));
    take_version_counter(ret, self);
    wrap_output(ret, std::move(flags), grad_fn);
    // --- BEGIN NEW CODE HERE ---
    if (jit::tracer::isTracing({self})) {
      jit::Node * n = jit::tracer::recordTrace("transpose", {self}, {ret});
      n->i_(jit::stringToSymbol("dim0"), dim0);
      n->i_(jit::stringToSymbol("dim1"), dim1);
    } 
    // --- END NEW CODE HERE ---
    return Tensor(std::move(ret));
}
For the JIT, we'll also have to code generate things that can translate back to ATen method invocations from this (I considered the design where we also record a lambda when we create a Node, but it's annoying to statically type this correctly, and it doesn't work if you have an optimization pass that creates fresh nodes.)

